  "answers": {
    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Direct experimental evidence that icariin (ICA) promotes osteogenic differentiation**\n\n- **Mouse MC3T3‑E1 pre‑osteoblasts** – ICA dose‑ and time‑dependently raised circRNA mmu_circ_0000349, JMJD3, ALP and RUNX2 expression and increased ALP activity and mineralized nodules; inhibition of the circRNA reversed these effects, implicating the mmu_circ_0000349/miR‑138‑5p/JMJD3 axis as a mechanistic driver of ICA‑induced osteogenesis【PMID:38045146】. *(in‑vitro, murine)*  \n- **LPS‑injured MC3T3‑E1 cells** – ICA restored ALP activity, RUNX2 protein, OPN and BMP2 levels, and rescued mineral nodule formation in a dose‑dependent manner; the effect was abolished by miR‑34c over‑expression, indicating ICA acts through down‑regulation of miR‑34c and inhibition of JNK/p38/NF‑κB signaling【PMID:27492554】. *(in‑vitro, murine)*  \n- **Human bone‑derived mesenchymal stem cells (hBMSCs)** – ICA enhanced ALP activity, BSP‑II and RUNX2 expression and suppressed adipogenic markers (PPARγ, C/EBPα); these outcomes required activation of Wnt/β‑catenin signalling and were blocked by miR‑23a over‑expression, establishing a miR‑23a‑mediated pathway for ICA‑driven osteogenesis【PMID:33743412】. *(in‑vitro, human)*  \n- **MC3T3‑E1 cells (miR‑153/Runx2 axis)** – ICA increased cell proliferation, ALP activity, mineralisation and up‑regulated miR‑153 and RUNX2; luciferase assays confirmed miR‑153 directly targets the Runx2 3′‑UTR, supporting a miR‑153/Runx2 mechanism【PMID:29904399】. *(in‑vitro, murine)*  \n- **Osteoporotic rat model + BMSC culture** – Oral ICA (or miR‑335‑5p over‑expression) restored bone micro‑architecture in ovariectomised rats and rescued BMSC mineralisation; ICA acted by up‑regulating miR‑335‑5p, which suppressed PTEN to promote osteogenesis【PMID:35292240】. *(in‑vivo, rodent + in‑vitro)*  \n\n**Associative observations / supporting trends**\n\n- ICA consistently up‑regulated canonical osteogenic markers (ALP, RUNX2, BMP2, OCN) across species and cell types, while down‑regulating osteoclast‑linked miRNAs (miR‑34c, miR‑138‑5p) or adipogenic cues (PPARγ).  \n- Protective effects were observed in stress models (LPS, Ti particles) where"
